vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/dovecot/core

Dovecot and Postfix — what was read, where, and on what date.

⚠ EVERY SETTING THE SLICE DEPENDS ON WAS RUN AGAINST THE REAL IMAGE BEFORE IT WAS WRITTEN DOWN, on
2026-09-23: docker.io/dovecot/dovecot:2.4.5 and :2.3.21.1, and Debian 13's postfix 3.10.13 in
deploy/images/mail-postfix. MailDataPlaneTests keeps the Dovecot half; MailDeliveryOnK3sTests the rest.

── passwd-file ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  user:password:uid:gid:gecos:home:shell:extra_fields

Six colons after the password. An EMPTY password field means no password check (Dovecot's
passwd-file documentation); `{CRYPT}!` is a hash no input produces, and `nologin=y` in the extra
fields refuses the login outright. Both are rendered for a mailbox with no passwordRef.

Per-mailbox quota, read back with `doveadm quota get -u` against each image:

  2.4.5     userdb_quota_storage_size=100M      — honoured ONLY when the domain's default is the
                                                  top-level quota_storage_size, not a setting inside
                                                  the quota "User quota" { } filter
  2.3.21.1  userdb_quota_rule=*:storage=100M

Each major ignores the other's field, so a mailbox writes both without knowing its domain's version.

── The password scheme ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  {SHA512-CRYPT}$6$rounds=100000$<16-char salt>$<86-char hash>

Verified by Dovecot 2.4.5 and 2.3.21.1 accepting a login against a hash Sha512Crypt produced, and by
OpenSSL 3.5.5's `openssl passwd -6` reproducing the specification's vectors.

── Postfix maps ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  virtual_mailbox_maps = texthash:/etc/postfix/mailboxes   — "{local}@{domain} OK" per *.passwd
  virtual_alias_maps   = texthash:/etc/postfix/virtual     — every *.virtual, then the catch-all

texthash needs no postmap and is read once per process, which is why the start script reloads on a
checksum change of the mounted files.
